<!-- ============================================================ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS -->
<section>
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">Section 06</div>
    <h2>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
    <p>Evidence-based insights for fuel pricing policy, consumer protection, and market transparency</p>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="finding-num">1</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div class="finding-tag">Price Levels</div>
      <h4>E10 petrol is the cheapest fuel at 145.2p/litre; B7_PREMIUM and HVO are 28% more expensive at ~187p/litre</h4>
      <p>The six-fuel price hierarchy is clear and consistent: E10 (145.2p) → E5 (161.3p) → B7_STANDARD (167.0p) → B7_PREMIUM (185.8p) ≈ HVO (187.4p). The standard diesel premium over standard petrol is 21.8p/litre — a meaningful ongoing cost burden for diesel vehicle owners and freight operators.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="finding-num">2</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div class="finding-tag">Consumer Price Disparity</div>
      <h4>B7_STANDARD diesel shows the widest price spread of main fuels: 33p IQR, meaning consumers risk paying up to £16.50 more per fill depending on forecourt choice</h4>
      <p>The 33p interquartile range for diesel is more than double E10's 16p IQR. For a 50-litre diesel fill, selecting a Q3-priced station over a Q1-priced station costs £16.50 more. This scale of disparity suggests reduced competition or geographic pricing segmentation in the diesel market, warranting further investigation under competition policy frameworks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="finding-num">3</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div class="finding-tag">Market Transparency</div>
      <h4>The majority of retailers (80.4%) update prices within 1 hour — the government open data scheme is broadly working as intended</h4>
      <p>With a system-wide median update lag of just ~3 minutes and 80.4% of records updated within an hour, the UK Fuel Finder open data scheme is delivering near real-time price transparency at the majority of forecourts. This is a positive baseline for consumer-facing comparison tools.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="finding-num">4</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div class="finding-tag">Compliance Concern</div>
      <h4>3.6% of records (1,802) have update lags exceeding 7 days; one record is 52 days stale — indicating a tail of non-compliant or malfunctioning reporters</h4>
      <p>While most retailers perform well, a persistent tail of stale reports undermines data reliability and consumer trust. Stations with lags exceeding 7 days should be identified and audited. The maximum observed lag of 1,263 hours (52.6 days) is particularly concerning and suggests systematic non-compliance at a small number of sites.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="finding-num">5</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div class="finding-tag">Data Integrity</div>
      <h4>1,011 records (2.0%) carry impossible negative update lags — a systemic data quality issue requiring investigation with the government feed operator</h4>
      <p>Records where source_updated_at post-dates recorded_at indicate either retailer-side clock errors, timestamp normalisation bugs in the data pipeline, or issues in the government Fuel Finder API. While the magnitude (maximum −0.999 hrs) is modest, the pattern affects 2% of the dataset and should be escalated as a data quality defect to fuelcosts.co.uk and the API operator.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="finding-num">6</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div class="finding-tag">Alternative Fuels</div>
      <h4>HVO and B10 have critically limited infrastructure: 52 and 33 stations respectively, with poorer reporting compliance than mainstream fuels</h4>
      <p>HVO (Hydrotreated Vegetable Oil) and B10 biodiesel are positioned as lower-carbon alternatives, but near-zero station coverage and higher reporting lag rates (HVO median lag: 1.02 hrs, only 49.5% within-1-hr vs. ~82% for mainstream fuels) make them impractical for most consumers. Policy action to expand infrastructure and improve reporting compliance could accelerate decarbonisation in the road transport sector.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

</section>

